  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA surface low continues to organize and deepen off the \nCarolina coast. This low will gradually ride up the coast\nthrough the day, eventually moving into the Gulf of Maine by\nlate tonight or early Monday morning. Not much has changed\nregarding our thinking as a surprising amount of uncertainty\nstill remains. Guidance continues to flip back and forth on\nwhether or not to track the low farther east. That being said, \nthere does seem to have been a little bit of an overall shift \neast than previous updates and now that we are in the range of\nthe higher resolution models, we should get a pretty good idea\nsoon if those trends are real. \n\nThe latest picture of NBM probabilities still suggest the \nheaviest rain totals will be over southeast New Hampshire and \ncoastal/interior Maine. Here, the chances for an inch of rain or\ngreater generally range from 50 to 80 percent. However, we do \nsee some 90+ percentiles over portions of the Midcoast. \nProbabilities decrease to the northwest. Far northern New \nHampshire and northwest Maine may see little to no rain given \nthe eastward trend. Probabilities for two inches or greater max\nout in the 40 to 60 percent range, mainly over coastal Maine \n(highest values over the Midcoast). This system will be moving \nfairly quickly so precipitation should mainly be out of the area\nby mid afternoon on Monday. \n\nRegarding temperatures, expect highs mainly in the 50s today but\na few lower 60s will be possible over coastal Maine and southern\nNew Hampshire. Highs on Monday will be a little bit warmer in\nadvance of an approaching cold front as clouds will be\nscattering out across the south. Values will range from the low\nto mid 50s north, to the low to mid 60s south and along the\ncoast.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nTuesday and Wednesday should be pretty quiet but the next\npotential coastal low will approach on Thanksgiving. Details\nremain sketchy with plenty of scenarios still on the table but\nmost of the global models and their respective ensembles suggest\nwe will another round of widespread precipitation Thursday\nevening through at least Friday night. There has been more a\ntrend towards a warmer solution so the best chances for wintry\nconditions may be confined to the higher elevations. NBM\nprobabilities continue to show the highest probabilities for an\ninch of liquid or greater over southeast New Hampshire and\ncoastal Maine, but considerable spread remains among the \nvarious models and their ensemble members.  \n\nThe other concern will be the coastal flood potential, as tides\nwill be astronomically high, and there will also be the \npotential for a long fetch/strong onshore winds given the \npressure gradient in place, in addition to rough surf/high \nwaves.",
